-- | The refusal for a form that has **no Rust expression spelling at all**.
-- |
-- | Four forms qualify, and plan 55 leaf 1 is where they stopped being
-- | impossible by construction: collapsing `StmtKind` into `Expr` means the
-- | type no longer keeps a `let`, a let-else, a binding `await` or a `with`
-- | binder out of an expression position. The first three are *statements* in
-- | Rust; the fourth lowers to a callback whose body is every element after
-- | it, so a nested one would quietly produce an empty closure.
-- |
-- | # Why `compile_error!` and not a Redox diagnostic
-- |
-- | A diagnostic would need a code, a trigger and a `tests/ui/` fixture, and
-- | **there is no trigger**: `prefix` has no production for any of the four,
-- | so no `.sdx` reaches this. A code nothing can raise is the rot
-- | `crates/redox-cli/tests/code_coverage.rs` exists to prevent.
-- |
-- | `compile_error!` is the answer the emitter already gives for an operator
-- | that parses and has no lowering — see `emit_expr`'s `BinaryOp`
-- | fallthrough. It keeps `redox-fuzz`'s invariant true, because this *is*
-- | Rust `syn` parses, and it fails loudly where a `todo!()` would fail at run
-- | time and a plausible-looking lowering would not fail at all. Plan 07's
-- | remapping lands rustc's report on the `.sdx` line, so the author sees
-- | their own source either way.
export(crate) unlowerable : &str -> String
unlowerable what =
  format! \-> "compile_error!(\"#{what} is a statement in Rust and has no expression spelling; this is a Redox defect, not a defect in your program\")"
